    "product_id": "92462810-bc4a-4cee-be90-5a64ca78c3af",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 161122\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n422 AM MST Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Wind advisories in effect today through Saturday morning across\n  southern Arizona and southeast California, with breezy to windy\n  conditions expected as forecast peak wind gusts reach up to \n  40-55 mph.\n\n- Strong winds on today will likely lead to widespread lofted \n  dust, leading to hazy conditions, and localized dust channels \n  occasionally reducing visibility below one mile.\n\n- Dry weather with near normal temperatures the next couple days,\n  then slightly cooler with a slight chances for rain showers this\n  weekend.\n  \n- A Pacific low pressure system moving down the coast early next\n  week is currently forecast to bring regional rain chances, \n  breezy conditions, and cooler temperatures by the middle to \n  latter portions of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nMid-level WV imagery and RAP40 500mb analysis show the cut off low \nnow developed over southern California and northern Baja, while high \npressure continues to strengthen over NW CONUS. The high pressure \nwill be be the driving force for temperatures remaining near normal \ntoday. Though the core of the ridge is to the north of the state, H5 \nheights will still reach between 585-588 dam over the desert SW. As \nmentioned, near normal afternoon highs can be expected today with \ntemperatures in the upper 80s to low 90s in the western lower \ndeserts and along the Colorado River Valley, while south-central AZ \nwill maintain highs in the mid to upper 80s. The upper level ridge \nwill quickly pass and move into the Plains by Saturday, allowing \nheights aloft to dampen and be influenced by the lingering cut off \nlow. This will result in Saturday cooling into the upper 70s to low \n80s. This will also be in conjunction with increasing cloud coverage \nand about a 10-20% chances of light showers/virga across south-\ncentral AZ and the eastern high terrain as early as Saturday \nafternoon. \n\nOtherwise, the main weather concern will be winds due to a strong \npressure gradient. This may be one of the the strongest easterly \ngradient wind events of the last decade. With strong winds lofted \nand blowing dust also poses a threat, especially across dust prone \nareas. Northerly winds gusts have already developed in the SE CA and \nalong the Colorado River Valley and are under a Wind Advisory \nthrough Friday afternoon, where peak gusts between 40-45 mph can be \nexpected. Over the next several hours this morning the surface high \nwill migrate from southern Montana into the southern Plains/southern \nRockies region and the pressure gradient will continue to strengthen \nand shifting the winds from the north to out of the east. Near \nsunrise this morning the Wind Advisory will expand eastwards into SW \nand SC Arizona, where gusts between 45-50 mph will be possible, \nwhile the Phoenix Metro will be slightly lower with gusts between 35-\n45 mph. Winds this strong are likely to pick up a good amount of \ndust, creating hazy conditions, and will likely lead to some dust \nchannels developing, with reduction in visibilities to under 1 mile \nat times, depending on land use. Most of the region will be under \nadvisory level by mid afternoon today, while Pinal County and \nsouthern Gila County will continue with strong gusts through the \novernight periods into late Saturday morning, with locally up to 55 \nmph over mountain peaks.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nThe strong pressure gradient and resultant strong easterly winds \nacross southern AZ will weaken heading into Sunday as the strong \nsurface high in the Plains weakens and shifts to the Mid-Atlantic.\nWinds early Sunday morning will remain elevated over south-\ncentral Arizona and the higher terrain with gusts between 20-25 in\nSC AZ, and 30-35 mph in the higher terrain. Past sunrise these \nwinds will quickly weaken as the upper level pattern continues to \nquickly shift. The ridge will continue to push eastwards, and the \ncutoff low will begin to weaken and become an open wave, lifting \nthrough the region Sunday- Monday. As the open wave moves drifts \nthrough, lingering light showers/virga remain in the forecast, \n20-35% Sunday, then 10-20% Monday. This will be confined to the \nhigher terrain areas, where with enough extra lift may be possible\nfor showers to develop. However, if any activity does develop \ntotal accumulations are expected to be only a few hundredths, or \nnear zero. For the lower deserts temperatures are expected to \nrebound back into the mid to upper 80s Sunday and into early next \nweek.\n\nBy late Monday into Tuesday a troughing feature will begin to dive \ndown the western coastline, however confidence is low in the timing \nand strength of the feature. This has resulted in a decent amount of \nspread in for temperatures by mid next week. As it stands now temperatures\nwill remain near to slightly below normal Monday-Tuesday then start\nto fall 6-9 degrees below normal for the later half of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1115Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nThe main aviation weather concerns during the upcoming TAF period\nwill be gusty to at times strong easterly winds and areas of\nlofted dust causing slantwise visibility issues. Winds currently\nare elevated between 10-15 kts with areas of LLWS possible through\nsunrise. Thereafter, gusts will pick up significantly after 15-16z\nin excess of 25 kts, with potential peak gusts as high as 30-35\nkts possible sometime between the 16-20z timeframe. The strong \nwind gusts will likely result in areas of BLDU, which could result\nin temporary reductions in visibilities to sub-VFR conditions. \nMuch greater confidence exists in lofted dust resulting in \nslantwise visibility issues through much of the daylight hours. \nClear skies will prevail through this afternoon before mid to high\nclouds spread over the region by the evening hours. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nBreezy conditions through this afternoon will be the main aviation\nconcern during the upcoming TAF period. North-northwest flow to\nnortheast flow will be common throughout the period, with periodic\ngusts upwards of 25 kts, at times higher, at KBLH and slightly\nweaker at KIPL. Mostly clear skies this morning will give way to \nincreasing mid to high clouds by late morning through the \nafternoon at KIPL and by this evening at KBLH. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air will persist over the region over the next couple of days \nwith mostly clear skies and seasonally low humidities. MinRHs \nwill be between 15-30% across the region through Saturday. Winds\nwill turn easterly over the morning, becoming stronger tonight \nthrough Saturday across southern AZ. Winds will be strongest over \nmountain tops during the overnight and early morning hours \ntomorrow and Saturday, with gusts up to 50-55 mph. Strong gusty \nwinds will spread more to the lower deserts during the daylight \nhours and be strongest in the morning through early afternoon \ntoday, with gusts up to 40-45 mph. These strong easterlies and \n20-25% RHs may still create locally elevated fire weather \nconditions midday through the afternoon tomorrow, but fuels are \nnot the most receptive after recent rains. The easterly breezes \nwill not be nearly as strong during the day Saturday and will \ntrend down further into Sunday. Light showers are possible this \nweekend, but the chance for wetting rain is low (<10%). A Pacific \ntrough is expected to bring regional rain chances, breezy winds, \nand cooler temperatures next week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...     Wind Advisory until 11 AM MST Saturday for AZZ547-552-553-555-\n     557-558-560>562.\n\n     Wind Advisory until 2 PM MST this afternoon for AZZ530.\n\n     Wind Advisory until 3 PM MST this afternoon for AZZ531>534-536-\n     538-539-554-559.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 8 AM this morning to 3 PM MST this afternoon \n     for AZZ537-540>546-548>551.\n\nCA...Wind Advisory until 2 PM PDT this afternoon for CAZ564-565-\n     568>570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Ryan \nAVIATION...Lojero/RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Ryan\n",
